{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AiJHZnK8GS2LAhNXMJbm6f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCITATION RULES — KB-FIRST TOKEN DOCUMENTS\n2\t\n3\tGoal\n4\t- Define how documents that use a KB as primary source represent and use citations when working with KB and external sources.\n5\t- Preserve token-style citations needed for downstream audit and compile workflows.\n6\t\n7\tCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n8\t- Use TOKEN citations in research outputs; do NOT use [@Key] here.\n9\t- Supported tokens:\n10\t  - Knowledge Base documents: `[KB:filename.md]` (for each .md file in the KB that contributes information).\n11\t  - External web sources: `[WEB:https://full-url]`.\n12\t  - ArXiv preprints: `[ARXIV:paper-id]`.\n13\t  - DOI-identified works: `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n14\t  - Optional Zotero items: `[ZOTERO:item_key]` when a Zotero MCP is used.\n15\t- Do **not** convert token citations to [@Key] in research workflows.\n16\t- Do **not** remove any valid citation tokens that appear in the draft.\n17\t- Reasoning: token citations are required for downstream audit verification and for later conversion to [@Key] during compile/audit/enhance.\n18\t\n19\tPlacement Rules\n20\t- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support.\n21\t- Multiple citations are allowed, e.g.:\n22\t  - `... [KB:doc1.md][WEB:https://...]`.\n23\t  - `... [KB:hazard.md][DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n24\t- Never place citation tokens inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n25\t- Clearly separate KB vs external evidence across the document.\n26\t\n27\tKB vs WEB Evidence Policy (RESEARCH)\n28\t- Prefer **KB content** over external sources where possible.\n29\t- Treat KB documents as the primary source of truth for answering the task, when they are relevant.\n30\t- Use external sources only when one of these conditions holds:\n31\t  - The KB does **not** contain the required information (gaps).\n32\t  - KB content is **ambiguous or conflicting** and needs clarification.\n33\t  - The task explicitly asks for **external benchmarking or comparison** (e.g., \"compare with recent standards\" or \"benchmark against international practice\").\n34\t- When KB already provides a sufficient, unambiguous answer, it is **not necessary** to add redundant WEB/DOI/ARXIV citations.\n35\t\n36\tCoverage Rules\n37\t- For every KB document you draw information from, include at least one `[KB:filename.md]` citation where its information is used.\n38\t- For every substantial answer subsection derived from KB, ensure **at least one** KB token is present if KB contains relevant information.\n39\t- Use external citations `[WEB:/ARXIV:/DOI:]` to:\n40\t  - Fill genuine gaps in KB coverage.\n41\t  - Resolve conflicts between KB sources.\n42\t  - Provide explicit benchmarks or external comparisons requested by the task.\n43\t\n44\tMinimum External Citations (Soft Rule)\n45\t- When **real gaps** require external support, aim for at least 3 authoritative external citations across the document.\n46\t- If the KB fully answers the task and no meaningful gaps exist, it is acceptable to have **zero** external citations.\n47\t\n48\tDeduplication\n49\t- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim.\n50\t- If multiple tokens cite the same underlying source, keep one representative token in the paragraph.\n51\t- Avoid spamming multiple WEB/DOI/ARXIV tokens that do not add distinct evidence.\n52\t\n53\tEnforcement Notes\n54\t- Documents that use KB material but provide no `[KB:...]` citations should be treated as non-compliant with these rules.\n55\t- External citations should be **authoritative and traceable** (URLs resolvable; DOIs/arXiv IDs valid) and reasonably recent when the domain requires up-to-date information.\n56\t- Downstream audit rules (rules/audit.md) may use these citation tokens as evidence hooks to judge veracity.\n57\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bfbc9bc9-d8c5-4165-9dda-26a80cc913ad","uuid":"81f29a3d-b0db-481c-92a2-f7afacd756a0","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:32:45.843Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/citations.research.md","content":"CITATION RULES — KB-FIRST TOKEN DOCUMENTS\n\nGoal\n- Define how documents that use a KB as primary source represent and use citations when working with KB and external sources.\n- Preserve token-style citations needed for downstream audit and compile workflows.\n\nCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n- Use TOKEN citations in research outputs; do NOT use [@Key] here.\n- Supported tokens:\n  - Knowledge Base documents: `[KB:filename.md]` (for each .md file in the KB that contributes information).\n  - External web sources: `[WEB:https://full-url]`.\n  - ArXiv preprints: `[ARXIV:paper-id]`.\n  - DOI-identified works: `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n  - Optional Zotero items: `[ZOTERO:item_key]` when a Zotero MCP is used.\n- Do **not** convert token citations to [@Key] in research workflows.\n- Do **not** remove any valid citation tokens that appear in the draft.\n- Reasoning: token citations are required for downstream audit verification and for later conversion to [@Key] during compile/audit/enhance.\n\nPlacement Rules\n- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support.\n- Multiple citations are allowed, e.g.:\n  - `... [KB:doc1.md][WEB:https://...]`.\n  - `... [KB:hazard.md][DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n- Never place citation tokens inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented).\n- Clearly separate KB vs external evidence across the document.\n\nKB vs WEB Evidence Policy (RESEARCH)\n- Prefer **KB content** over external sources where possible.\n- Treat KB documents as the primary source of truth for answering the task, when they are relevant.\n- Use external sources only when one of these conditions holds:\n  - The KB does **not** contain the required information (gaps).\n  - KB content is **ambiguous or conflicting** and needs clarification.\n  - The task explicitly asks for **external benchmarking or comparison** (e.g., \"compare with recent standards\" or \"benchmark against international practice\").\n- When KB already provides a sufficient, unambiguous answer, it is **not necessary** to add redundant WEB/DOI/ARXIV citations.\n\nCoverage Rules\n- For every KB document you draw information from, include at least one `[KB:filename.md]` citation where its information is used.\n- For every substantial answer subsection derived from KB, ensure **at least one** KB token is present if KB contains relevant information.\n- Use external citations `[WEB:/ARXIV:/DOI:]` to:\n  - Fill genuine gaps in KB coverage.\n  - Resolve conflicts between KB sources.\n  - Provide explicit benchmarks or external comparisons requested by the task.\n\nMinimum External Citations (Soft Rule)\n- When **real gaps** require external support, aim for at least 3 authoritative external citations across the document.\n- If the KB fully answers the task and no meaningful gaps exist, it is acceptable to have **zero** external citations.\n\nDeduplication\n- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim.\n- If multiple tokens cite the same underlying source, keep one representative token in the paragraph.\n- Avoid spamming multiple WEB/DOI/ARXIV tokens that do not add distinct evidence.\n\nEnforcement Notes\n- Documents that use KB material but provide no `[KB:...]` citations should be treated as non-compliant with these rules.\n- External citations should be **authoritative and traceable** (URLs resolvable; DOIs/arXiv IDs valid) and reasonably recent when the domain requires up-to-date information.\n- Downstream audit rules (rules/audit.md) may use these citation tokens as evidence hooks to judge veracity.\n","numLines":57,"startLine":1,"totalLines":57}}}
